uuid "39027322-ce0d-5254-be7a-01d2a2205ede" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162989 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163277 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now consider Jordan canonical forms of $3 \\times 3$ matrices.\n\nLet $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The simplest case is when $A$ has three distinct eigenvalues, say $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2,$ and  $\\lambda_3.$ In this case, $A$ must be diagonalizable. Therefore, the Jordan canonical form of $A$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis matrix has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=8,$ $\\lambda_2=5,$ and $\\lambda_3=3.$ Therefore, its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRemember that a Jordan canonical form of a matrix is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163277 #edb/ref 1065 5464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163277 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6659f1d2-f6c2-5a21-815e-c72ad939472d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163277 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163278 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n(\\lambda-8)^2(\\lambda - 3) = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this matrix has only two distinct eigenvalues, namely,\n\n- $\\lambda=8$ of algebraic multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $\\lambda=3$ of algebraic multiplicity $1.$\n\nHow can we find its Jordan canonical form?\n\nIn this example, we have one $1 \\times 1$ Jordan block $J_1(3)$ corresponding to $\\lambda=3.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda=8{:}$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2 \\times 2$ Jordan block $J_2(8).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=8$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A-8I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A-8I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1 \\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely $J_1(8) {\\:\\oplus\\:} J_1(8).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=8$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A-8I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A-8I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda=8.$\n\nComputing $A-8I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 9\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}\\leftrightarrow {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A-8I)=1,$ there will be only one eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=8,$ say, $\\mathbf{v}_1.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=8$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A-8I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A-8I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case I*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs always, the Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163278 #edb/ref 1065 5465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163278 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "630b8bf4-5ce6-5f2a-ac92-8ba6290b80ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163278 #edb/ref 1067 "Jordan Canonical Forms of 3x3 Matrices That Have Two Distinct Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163279 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose we are given  a non-scalar ![](images/59/599d5897394ff1110f6a3bac7053c6836d231de17effad76a1c633d1aed81b33.svg) matrix that has only one eigenvalue ![](images/98/98a445be63a324ba80515c96b7f891797392bdeafb375acb75ce1613f4475d4c.svg) of algebraic multiplicity ![](images/9f/9fc073d1e6ebde82eb949a875e40be00b9196e9b14473ee13fa47a26a98d8450.svg) \n\nThen, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to ![](images/6e/6edf088394f33f03a13a73707848c74294779fcc01682668ca72e0426f8f5414.svg) \n\n- *Case I.* We may have the single ![](images/59/599d5897394ff1110f6a3bac7053c6836d231de17effad76a1c633d1aed81b33.svg) Jordan block ![](images/f6/f6d9095ffd16744855059e291e3cc87902a59c046fc750126ef02239ccd092c0.svg) This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ![](images/ea/ea09b6c395a529e8e8ea2350c1b8a81394a870e9ded424f3e192bde9e4dd7ebe.svg) and ![](images/de/de50c883471601a0c2877f85ec01398448d768907b984e48f4ed6dfa642cb7ce.svg) corresponding to ![](images/98/98a445be63a324ba80515c96b7f891797392bdeafb375acb75ce1613f4475d4c.svg) form the following chain:\n![](images/84/84c1eae53f974301449d14925df28e015f4fbe89f5d7a9138e0552a3e4396ee8.svg)\n\n- *Case II.* We may have a composition of one ![](images/25/2521651122403a440e682fef383af333958f5b0d1334abe8b0771865d4daf100.svg) and one ![](images/1c/1c187df58a38f1b82665e25c048f9717ef4287a71ae05fce51c1e4d612c264d2.svg) Jordan block, say ![](images/09/09f7fbbc1c53852939a487bc4d96878c7f6a87051d88a20cc75dba1da78fe641.svg) This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ![](images/ea/ea09b6c395a529e8e8ea2350c1b8a81394a870e9ded424f3e192bde9e4dd7ebe.svg) and ![](images/de/de50c883471601a0c2877f85ec01398448d768907b984e48f4ed6dfa642cb7ce.svg) corresponding to ![](images/98/98a445be63a324ba80515c96b7f891797392bdeafb375acb75ce1613f4475d4c.svg) form the following chains:\n![](images/c8/c828d294e33c125560da89a1a3cecdb0abddac7d1ae9ca370c31a7fa0ba21b32.svg)\n\nTo illustrate this situation, let's consider the matrix \n![](images/a3/a300eae1cbda5115e62c726f522e59a8fd0b705f581c435aaa3d861bcc3d8c6a.svg)\nwhich has eigenvalues ![](images/b5/b567e00c3d487220a9685f77f9d5c0eb6c5c33090867ac432d4c14430abd26c3.svg) \n\nTo determine which of the above cases applies to this example, we now find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to  ![](images/c5/c5a00088d2fcc7951b02f0291e50136dd7e4bd2a53f188dfc498645eb26929aa.svg) \n\nComputing ![](images/5a/5a20a88adadea894f5c2f3a53069a68f862257ac836b14b82b5d74110a196563.svg) we get\n\n![](images/ea/eac32a1ee692cfcd129f27726e8e081af04bd2badea33f9b72e9a7cba5f48ae9.svg)\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n![](images/e3/e33a312ef5059b0d75f8df88819dc318075b82e85eeeef34d942a1229b9d1831.svg)\n\nIn the matrix above, there is only one pivot column (the ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg)st one). So, the rank of ![](images/0d/0dc4be531ef1f00ddf4647f4f6c4f7970b3b8a21afcbcab063906c13a00f6b0f.svg) is ![](images/da/daae2fd3251c4ba175906af980d1c33d4db057c616509ec6a146307d64417228.svg) Using the rank-nullity theorem, we have that \n\n![](images/6f/6f04fb2264dc56bec28dc627102a51e4cc5755ed391fbe153e61db8481973a02.svg) \n\nRecall that the nullity of ![](images/0d/0dc4be531ef1f00ddf4647f4f6c4f7970b3b8a21afcbcab063906c13a00f6b0f.svg) gives the number of eigenvectors corresponding to ![](images/c5/c5a00088d2fcc7951b02f0291e50136dd7e4bd2a53f188dfc498645eb26929aa.svg) Therefore, there must be two such eigenvectors, say, ![](images/ed/ed99403d5ac94993d00ca1f9c99def2e8d3bcbddba68d8aa1c1a8651fd140e2a.svg) and ![](images/c4/c431b5221d32ff502ed5b01961391f2475e7436db7c773d078b132cbef6ee1d5.svg) \nSo, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue ![](images/a4/a4d92774d394fdeac65021885a694a2b47df3fb402e3c864a13db72886b8c180.svg) form the following chains:\n\n![](images/4b/4b901462508191e9f6da3dcf750dd8001f5851decb857e5d384491e3486a2d5b.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) has the following Jordan canonical decomposition:\n\n![](images/3b/3b39d39e3b497abdbb7564a8a7c5d9d4dee2f67061554b9a439c6a00b2b1c8a4.svg)\n\nwhich, as we know,  is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163279 #edb/ref 1065 5466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163279 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "12939654-040d-592d-aa5d-5cb46fd5662b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163279 #edb/ref 1067 "Jordan Canonical Forms of 3x3 Matrices That Have Only One Eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163280 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, there are ![](images/37/37e17c4a2302e6352fb7186621c9c43bb5e99d43a8a0bc700c0181d36c6cc915.svg) possible Jordan canonical forms ![](images/70/70f3dc39744c98269cbe57ad0ff14875c1f82614dfcaa8f7199a7a3c9ae9afcf.svg) for ![](images/59/599d5897394ff1110f6a3bac7053c6836d231de17effad76a1c633d1aed81b33.svg) matrices up to permutations of its Jordan blocks. \n\n- If ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) has *three* distinct eigenvalues ![](images/9d/9d0c15cb24551328e2d0f10079a7721ff2a7fd1ee1eb37821ceea5ab3dc7c872.svg)  and ![](images/ef/ef22b02965e0413d09c24e06011b23ba8400dfd6f4748bdcbdc14dc0b80af8d0.svg) then ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is diagonalizable, and its corresponding Jordan canonical form ![](images/70/70f3dc39744c98269cbe57ad0ff14875c1f82614dfcaa8f7199a7a3c9ae9afcf.svg) is simply the diagonal form of the matrix. In this case, ![](images/70/70f3dc39744c98269cbe57ad0ff14875c1f82614dfcaa8f7199a7a3c9ae9afcf.svg) is a composition of three ![](images/1c/1c187df58a38f1b82665e25c048f9717ef4287a71ae05fce51c1e4d612c264d2.svg) distinct Jordan blocks:\n![](images/94/941acf517462d22daf25ba674d4de785dfe7361afeab6ce5c7d49fa36dc85b7d.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) has *two* distinct eigenvalues ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg)  and ![](images/b8/b8ed089264b88bdcf4018f44db6dade448063b56a6ded558fb74d7c2ca61c0de.svg) where ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) has algebraic multiplicity ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg), then there are two possible Jordan canonical forms for ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg)\n\n- If there are two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg), then ![](images/70/70f3dc39744c98269cbe57ad0ff14875c1f82614dfcaa8f7199a7a3c9ae9afcf.svg) is the composition of two ![](images/1c/1c187df58a38f1b82665e25c048f9717ef4287a71ae05fce51c1e4d612c264d2.svg) Jordan blocks corresponding to ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and a ![](images/1c/1c187df58a38f1b82665e25c048f9717ef4287a71ae05fce51c1e4d612c264d2.svg) Jordan block corresponding to ![](images/fb/fbb2ca63da31ba7105fd44e63c44e63bc50de624a639331001cae14f72c5bf50.svg)\n![](images/2c/2cbe20384d702937e06cc00604d9d961434c7f56d5f7d60e9de5b20abba7e87e.svg)\n\n- If there is only one linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg), then ![](images/70/70f3dc39744c98269cbe57ad0ff14875c1f82614dfcaa8f7199a7a3c9ae9afcf.svg) is the composition of a ![](images/25/2521651122403a440e682fef383af333958f5b0d1334abe8b0771865d4daf100.svg) Jordan block corresponding to ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and a ![](images/1c/1c187df58a38f1b82665e25c048f9717ef4287a71ae05fce51c1e4d612c264d2.svg) Jordan block corresponding to ![](images/fb/fbb2ca63da31ba7105fd44e63c44e63bc50de624a639331001cae14f72c5bf50.svg)\n![](images/26/26ee966b717db6c1b9097774e8369ddb1ec3a16fc5d4a33b802be0beeb0b85bd.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) has only *one* eigenvalue ![](images/8f/8fdacc824812b8ac4fb3e0ac260f1600d35c084e1cb054d8c29e1ae43e2eb288.svg)  then there are three possible Jordan canonical forms for ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg)\n\n- If there are three linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to ![](images/98/98a445be63a324ba80515c96b7f891797392bdeafb375acb75ce1613f4475d4c.svg), then ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) must be a scalar matrix (meaning that it's already diagonalized), and ![](images/02/021e3f544ba10c7b968cffdc292f2c34de5d8ee021cebebb6784591c3f164490.svg) is a composition of three identical ![](images/1c/1c187df58a38f1b82665e25c048f9717ef4287a71ae05fce51c1e4d612c264d2.svg) Jordan blocks:\n![](images/36/36b20285662cfc7351fb06e831723fecc68d2958c23df068770e5f51a8ed6fc2.svg)\n\n- If there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to ![](images/98/98a445be63a324ba80515c96b7f891797392bdeafb375acb75ce1613f4475d4c.svg), then ![](images/70/70f3dc39744c98269cbe57ad0ff14875c1f82614dfcaa8f7199a7a3c9ae9afcf.svg) is the composition of a ![](images/25/2521651122403a440e682fef383af333958f5b0d1334abe8b0771865d4daf100.svg) Jordan block corresponding to ![](images/98/98a445be63a324ba80515c96b7f891797392bdeafb375acb75ce1613f4475d4c.svg) and a ![](images/1c/1c187df58a38f1b82665e25c048f9717ef4287a71ae05fce51c1e4d612c264d2.svg) Jordan block also corresponding to ![](images/6e/6edf088394f33f03a13a73707848c74294779fcc01682668ca72e0426f8f5414.svg)\n![](images/1b/1b46d0665442637c6e96d932ff408a2cd897386c6af36f2e2d63914ccdb2e32c.svg)\n\n- Finally,  if there is just one linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to ![](images/98/98a445be63a324ba80515c96b7f891797392bdeafb375acb75ce1613f4475d4c.svg), then ![](images/70/70f3dc39744c98269cbe57ad0ff14875c1f82614dfcaa8f7199a7a3c9ae9afcf.svg) is a single  ![](images/59/599d5897394ff1110f6a3bac7053c6836d231de17effad76a1c633d1aed81b33.svg) Jordan block corresponding to ![](images/6e/6edf088394f33f03a13a73707848c74294779fcc01682668ca72e0426f8f5414.svg)\n![](images/bd/bd0de3c8728e2577aeabce103de6f135589ecfcb285d75b3fcf890cabb826a72.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163280 #edb/ref 1065 5467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163280 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "517e4f7e-e3cb-5a02-b6da-c34cee1345b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163280 #edb/ref 1067 "Overview of All Possible Jordan Canonical Forms of 3x3 Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
